@startuml
' AsciiDoc → DOCX conversion pipeline comparison
' Shows why pandoc-direct was rejected in favor of docbook intermediate

skinparam activityBackgroundColor #F8F9FA
skinparam activityBorderColor #888888

|Assembler|
start
:Assembled AsciiDoc\n(stdin);

fork
  |Rejected: pandoc-direct|
  :pandoc -f asciidoc -t docx;
  note right #FCE4EC
    Pandoc's native AsciiDoc reader
    does not support:
    • asciidoctor-kroki diagrams
    • custom [item] block macros
    • Antora xref: macros
    → Broken output
  end note
  :❌ DOCX with missing diagrams\nand mangled macros;

fork again
  |Accepted: docbook pipeline|
  :asciidoctor -b docbook\n-r asciidoctor-kroki\n-a kroki-default-format=png;
  note right #E8F5E9
    DocBook is asciidoctor's native
    structured output. All features
    preserved:
    • Kroki diagrams → embedded images
    • xrefs → DocBook xref elements
    • Admonitions → DocBook admonitions
    • Tables → CALS tables
  end note
  :DocBook XML\n(stdout);
  :pandoc -f docbook -t docx;
  note right #E8F5E9
    Pandoc's docbook reader is mature.
    Converts:
    • xref → Word cross-references
    • images → embedded in docx
    • TOC → Word TOC field
    • SVG images → PNG via rsvg-convert
  end note
  :✓ well-formed DOCX\nwith navigable TOC,\ncross-references,\nand embedded images;

endfork

stop

@enduml
